# Meltdown — Audio

The game plays ten cues, one per event. This file defines the cues and what mute
does.

## The cues

| Cue | The event it answers |
| --- | --- |
| `fire` | An emitter's shot resolves. |
| `trip` | An emitter trips. |
| `death` | A surge unit's hp reaches `0`. |
| `leak` | A surge unit reaches its assigned exhaust. |
| `place` | A tower is placed on the floor. |
| `sell` | A placed tower is sold. |
| `wave-clear` | A wave clears. |
| `victory` | The victory screen opens. |
| `game-over` | The game-over screen opens. |
| `menu` | A menu highlight moves. |

Each cue is short and distinct from the other nine.

A cue is raised by the frame that resolves the event it answers, and it is played
from the frame loop, so a cue always names one real frame: the frame the shot
landed on, the frame the wave cleared on, the frame the tower was placed on. An
event that does not resolve raises nothing, so a placement refused as invalid
plays no `place` cue.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
## Playing them

Audio is synthesized with the Web Audio API. The build ships no audio file and
loads none.

Browsers block audio until the page has been interacted with, so the game plays
nothing at all before the first input reaches it, and it starts and runs
correctly whether or not audio can start.

## Mute

The build owns the mute bit. It is toggled by the `mute` action and by the
panel's mute control, from any screen, and `specs/hud.md` states how the control
reads.

While muted, none of the ten cues produces any sound. The game stays fully
playable muted: nothing about the floor, the run, or the screens changes with the
mute bit.
{{else}}
## Playing them

The cues are defined once, when the game is initialized, and played through the
{{engine.name}} engine's audio bus, which the engine documents in the `engine/`
directory at the root of this repository. Each cue is synthesized rather than
loaded: the build ships no audio file.

The engine owns the first-interaction unlock, so the game plays nothing before
the first input reaches it and it starts and runs correctly whether or not audio
can start.

## Mute

The engine owns the mute bit. It is toggled by the `mute` action and by the
panel's mute control, from any screen, and `specs/hud.md` states how the control
reads.

While muted, none of the ten cues produces any sound. The game stays fully
playable muted: nothing about the floor, the run, or the screens changes with the
mute bit.
{{/if}}
